From: ScorchDroid <noreply@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] android: the remaining missing visual events

Four hooks found by auditing upstream's client rendering tree against
this port, rather than by noticing something looked wrong. Each is the
usual shape: an #else beside an existing #ifndef S3D_SERVER, pushing an
event and nothing more.

- **Explosion.cpp, mushroom cloud.** Upstream raises a cloud sprite on a
  per-blast roll against the weapon's <createmushroomamount>, so the same
  nuke does not always produce one. The roll is reproduced rather than
  made unconditional - always-on would make every nuke identical.

- **Explosion.cpp, thrown debris.** Upstream flings opaque tumbling rock
  meshes alongside the fireball. Its count is
  5 + size * (ExplosionParticleMult / 40); that multiplier is a display
  option this port does not expose, so its default of 20 is used, and the
  total is capped so a large blast cannot flood the renderer.

- **TargetDamage.cpp, floating damage numbers.** Same gate as upstream
  (the target's own displayDamage flag) and the same +-2.5 scatter, so
  numbers from a multi-target blast do not stack on one spot.

- **PhysicsParticleObject.cpp, arena wall impact.** The wall side is
  carried in the event because the flash lies in that wall's plane. Worth
  knowing this is ordinary play, not a corner case: WallType defaults to
  WallRandom and this port's server config never sets it, so most rounds
  have walls.

- **TankSay.cpp, speech bubble.** Note this one also restores the *text*:
  upstream does both the bubble and the ChannelManager::showText call
  inside its client-only block, so before this a TankSay produced nothing
  at all here - neither bubble nor message.

One gate of upstream's is deliberately *not* carried over: the
!getServerMode() check that wraps both the damage number and the speech
bubble. Upstream is a client process that also runs a local server
context, and the check stops it doing the work twice. This port has only
the one context running these, and it is the server one - so keeping the
check meant neither effect was ever raised, while every ungated effect
worked, which is exactly the shape of bug that costs an afternoon.
host-tests pins both. The half of the gate that carries game meaning, the
target's own displayDamage flag, is kept exactly as upstream has it.

No game logic changes; every push is inside #ifdef S3D_SERVER or the
#else of an existing split.

Both text effects also read their position through getTargetPosition()
rather than upstream's getFloatPosition(). That float mirror is only
maintained when !serverMode_ (see TargetLife.cpp), so on this port it is
permanently (0,0,0), and every damage number was projected into the
corner of the map instead of over the target that was hurt. host-tests
assert the position, not just that the event fired, because "an event was
raised" was true the whole time this was broken.

diff --git a/src/common/actions/Explosion.cpp b/src/common/actions/Explosion.cpp
index 3f887050..6cebee92 100644
--- a/src/common/actions/Explosion.cpp
+++ b/src/common/actions/Explosion.cpp
@@ -98,6 +98,44 @@ void Explosion::init()
 		effect.b = colour[2].asFloat();
 		ScorchDroidEffects::push(effect);
 	}
+	// The mushroom cloud, for nuke-class weapons. Upstream raises it on a
+	// per-blast roll against <createmushroomamount> (Explosion.cpp's client
+	// body), so the same weapon does not always produce one - reproduced
+	// here rather than made unconditional, since always-on would make every
+	// nuke identical.
+	if (params_->getCreateMushroomAmount() > 0 &&
+		fixed::fromFloat(RAND) <= params_->getCreateMushroomAmount())
+	{
+		ScorchDroidEffects::EffectEvent cloud;
+		cloud.type = ScorchDroidEffects::eMushroom;
+		cloud.x = position_[0].asFloat();
+		cloud.y = position_[1].asFloat();
+		cloud.z = position_[2].asFloat();
+		// Upstream's own size for the cloud sprite.
+		cloud.size = params_->getSize().asFloat() - 2.0f;
+		ScorchDroidEffects::push(cloud);
+	}
+
+	// Thrown rock. Upstream's count is
+	// 5 + size * (ExplosionParticleMult / 40), where the multiplier is a
+	// display option this port does not expose; its default of 20 is used.
+	if (params_->getCreateDebris())
+	{
+		const float mult = 20.0f / 40.0f;
+		int debris = 5 + (int) (params_->getSize().asFloat() * mult);
+		if (debris > 40) debris = 40;
+		for (int d = 0; d < debris; d++)
+		{
+			ScorchDroidEffects::EffectEvent rock;
+			rock.type = ScorchDroidEffects::eDebris;
+			rock.x = position_[0].asFloat();
+			rock.y = position_[1].asFloat();
+			rock.z = position_[2].asFloat();
+			rock.size = params_->getSize().asFloat();
+			ScorchDroidEffects::push(rock);
+		}
+	}
+
 	// Muzzle smoke. Upstream raises this only for WeaponMuzzle - the puff a
 	// gun leaves when it fires, not something every explosion does - and
 	// does it right here, in the client body below (search addSmoke). Same
diff --git a/src/common/actions/TankSay.cpp b/src/common/actions/TankSay.cpp
index 8a34eb94..48561991 100644
--- a/src/common/actions/TankSay.cpp
+++ b/src/common/actions/TankSay.cpp
@@ -19,6 +19,11 @@
 ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
 
 #include <actions/TankSay.hpp>
+#ifdef S3D_SERVER
+	// Android build: the speech bubble is client-only here - see
+	// EffectEventQueue.h.
+	#include <EffectEventQueue.h>
+#endif
 #ifndef S3D_SERVER
 	#include <sprites/TalkRenderer.hpp>
 	#include <sprites/ExplosionTextures.hpp>
@@ -59,6 +64,30 @@ void TankSay::init()
 				white);
 			context_->getActionController().addAction(new SpriteAction(talk));
 
+			ChannelText text("general", text_);
+			text.setSrcPlayerId(playerId_);
+			ChannelManager::showText(*context_, text);
+		}
+#else
+		// Android build: the same speech bubble over the speaking tank,
+		// raised as an event. The text itself goes out on the general
+		// channel exactly as above - without this branch a TankSay produced
+		// nothing at all here, neither bubble nor message, since upstream
+		// does both inside the client-only block.
+		//
+		// No !getServerMode() check, unlike upstream: there it stops a
+		// client that also hosts from doing this twice, but this port runs
+		// these actions on the server context alone, so the check simply
+		// switched the bubble - and the bot's line in the chat log - off.
+		{
+			FixedVector turret = tank->getLife().getTankTurretPosition();
+			ScorchDroidEffects::EffectEvent bubble;
+			bubble.type = ScorchDroidEffects::eTalk;
+			bubble.x = turret[0].asFloat();
+			bubble.y = turret[1].asFloat();
+			bubble.z = turret[2].asFloat();
+			ScorchDroidEffects::push(bubble);
+
 			ChannelText text("general", text_);
 			text.setSrcPlayerId(playerId_);
 			ChannelManager::showText(*context_, text);
diff --git a/src/common/engine/PhysicsParticleObject.cpp b/src/common/engine/PhysicsParticleObject.cpp
index 459088e3..e2e09803 100644
--- a/src/common/engine/PhysicsParticleObject.cpp
+++ b/src/common/engine/PhysicsParticleObject.cpp
@@ -19,6 +19,11 @@
 ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
 
 #include <engine/PhysicsParticleObject.hpp>
+#ifdef S3D_SERVER
+	// Android build: the wall-hit flash is client-only here - see
+	// EffectEventQueue.h.
+	#include <EffectEventQueue.h>
+#endif
 #include <engine/ScorchedContext.hpp>
 #include <engine/ActionController.hpp>
 #include <engine/Simulator.hpp>
@@ -827,5 +832,28 @@ void PhysicsParticleActionObject::shotWallHit(CollisionInfo &collision)
 	{
 		addWallCollisionParticle(position_.asVector(), collision.collisionId);
 	}
+#else
+	// Android build: the same flash on the arena boundary, raised as an
+	// event. The wall side matters and is carried in `value` - the flash
+	// lies in that wall's plane, so without it the renderer cannot orient
+	// it. Walls are on in most rounds: WallType defaults to WallRandom, so
+	// this is ordinary play rather than a corner case.
+	if (!context_->getServerMode())
+	{
+		ScorchDroidEffects::EffectEvent hit;
+		hit.type = ScorchDroidEffects::eWallHit;
+		hit.x = position_[0].asFloat();
+		hit.y = position_[1].asFloat();
+		hit.z = position_[2].asFloat();
+		switch (collision.collisionId)
+		{
+		case CollisionIdWallLeft:   hit.value = (float) OptionsTransient::LeftSide;  break;
+		case CollisionIdWallRight:  hit.value = (float) OptionsTransient::RightSide; break;
+		case CollisionIdWallTop:    hit.value = (float) OptionsTransient::TopSide;   break;
+		case CollisionIdWallBottom: hit.value = (float) OptionsTransient::BotSide;   break;
+		default: return;
+		}
+		ScorchDroidEffects::push(hit);
+	}
 #endif
 }
diff --git a/src/common/target/TargetDamage.cpp b/src/common/target/TargetDamage.cpp
index cbc81867..bd05ded2 100644
--- a/src/common/target/TargetDamage.cpp
+++ b/src/common/target/TargetDamage.cpp
@@ -24,6 +24,10 @@
 #include <actions/Resurrection.hpp>
 #ifndef S3D_SERVER
 	#include <sprites/TextActionRenderer.hpp>
+#else
+	// Android build: the damage number's drawing half is client-only
+	// here - see EffectEventQueue.h.
+	#include <EffectEventQueue.h>
 #endif
 #include <common/OptionsScorched.hpp>
 #include <common/Defines.hpp>
@@ -171,6 +175,38 @@ void TargetDamage::damageTarget(ScorchedContext &context,
 				)
 			);
 		}
+#else
+		// Android build: the same floating damage number, raised as an event
+		// for the HUD to draw - this port has no in-world text, so it is
+		// projected and drawn in Compose alongside the name plates. Same
+		// gate as upstream (the target's own displayDamage flag) and the
+		// same scatter, so numbers from a multi-target blast do not stack
+		// on one spot.
+		//
+		// Upstream's own gate here is `!getServerMode() && displayDamage`.
+		// The serverMode half is dropped deliberately: upstream is a client
+		// process that also runs a local server context, and the check stops
+		// it drawing the number twice. This port has only the one context
+		// running damage, and it is the server one - so keeping the check
+		// meant the number was never raised at all. The target's own
+		// displayDamage flag, which is the half that carries game meaning,
+		// is kept exactly as upstream has it.
+		if (damagedTarget->getTargetState().getDisplayDamage())
+		{
+			// getTargetPosition(), not upstream's getFloatPosition(): the
+			// float mirror is only maintained when !serverMode_ (see
+			// TargetLife.cpp), so on this port - which runs the server
+			// context - it is permanently (0,0,0), and every number
+			// appeared in the corner of the map instead of over its target.
+			FixedVector &position = damagedTarget->getLife().getTargetPosition();
+			ScorchDroidEffects::EffectEvent number;
+			number.type = ScorchDroidEffects::eDamage;
+			number.x = position[0].asFloat() + RAND * 5.0f - 2.5f;
+			number.y = position[1].asFloat() + RAND * 5.0f - 2.5f;
+			number.z = position[2].asFloat() + RAND * 5.0f - 2.5f;
+			number.value = damage.asFloat();
+			ScorchDroidEffects::push(number);
+		}
 #endif // #ifndef S3D_SERVER
 
 		// Remove the life
